## The booking most families should make, and the one most of them do

The evening safari is what every listing sells. Pickup mid-afternoon, dune bashing, sunset stop, camp, dinner, show, back at the hotel somewhere around nine or ten at night.

For teenagers, that is the right booking. For a family with a four year old, it is seven to eight hours that ends with a tired child at a camp forty five minutes from the hotel.

The [morning desert safari](/desert-safari-dubai/morning-desert-safari/) is four to five hours. Pickup before dawn, dune bashing as the light comes up, camel ride, sandboarding, back at the hotel with the rest of the day intact. It is cooler, quieter and shorter, and from June to September it is the only version of this that is comfortable at all.

Most families who have done both say the same thing afterwards, which is that they wish they had done the morning one first.

## The arithmetic that changes at five people

Shared safaris charge **per person**. Private safaris charge **per vehicle**. That single difference decides the price for a family and almost no listing makes it obvious.

| Family size | Shared evening, per person | Private Land Cruiser, per vehicle |
|---|---|---|
| 2 people | AED 198 | AED 650 |
| 4 people | AED 396 | AED 650 |
| 6 people | AED 594 | AED 650 |

At two people, shared is the obvious answer. At six, the private vehicle costs AED 56 more and you travel alone, set your own pace and can ask the driver to stop.

With children, the pace part is worth more than it looks on paper. A shared car has five other guests in it who booked to be thrown around.

## What the children actually do

The dune bashing is a car ride. Children do not drive anything and do not need to be old enough for anything.

At the camp, the activities that land are the camel ride, the sandboarding and the show, in roughly that order. Sandboarding is the one that keeps them busy longest and it is included rather than charged. The fire and tanoura show holds attention better than the belly dance for younger children.

Quads are the exception, because they are the one thing a child actually rides. Ours start at **age six** in a fenced area on a 70 to 90cc machine, and the [safari package with a quad session](/desert-safari-dubai/quad-bike-desert-safari/) is AED 199 per person on the shared evening safari. If riding is what the children are looking forward to, book that package rather than hoping to add it at the camp.

## The thing to say when you book

Tell us if anyone in the group gets carsick, is pregnant, has a bad back, or simply does not want the hard version of the dune drive.

Our drivers will run a gentler line when they know in advance. In a shared car that is harder to arrange, because the other five guests booked for the opposite. This is the single most common reason a family comes back from a safari with one person who did not enjoy it, and it is a two second message to avoid.

The other thing worth saying is the ages. It changes which package we recommend and it changes the price, and finding out at the camp that a child is too young for the quad is a bad way to discover it.

## Timing, honestly

**October to April**, either slot works. Evening for the camp and the show, morning for empty dunes and photographs.

**June to September**, take the morning. An afternoon in the open desert in a Dubai summer is genuinely uncomfortable, and children lose patience with it before adults do. The sand looks identical in the photographs either way.

Hotel pickup inside Dubai is free on every booking. Transfers from Sharjah, Ajman, Abu Dhabi and Ras Al Khaimah are quoted with the booking, and from those distances the drive is long enough that the morning safari is worth the early start.

## What it costs to get it right

[Every safari price is on one page](/desert-safari-dubai/price/), shared and private, with the price basis on each row so you can do the per-person maths yourself.

Nothing is paid when you book. You settle at the base before you travel, and if wind, heat or visibility make the route unsafe we contact you before you leave the hotel and reschedule at no cost.

We have run this base since 2020 and taken more than 17,000 guests out. Families are the group we get asked about most, and the pattern in the ones that go well is always the same: shorter than they expected to book, and told us the ages in advance.
